#
# Security region: files, hardened
#
# What a tool does to a path it was given. STRICT_FILES 2: mkdir, mkfifo,
# mknod, install and cp refuse to make an entry in a world-writable directory
# without the sticky bit, cp and every output open replace a link at the
# destination rather than write through it, cp -r builds a directory in a
# private stage and publishes it whole, chown -R -L follows no link below the
# operand, and a security.* attribute in an archive is not applied. The
# fs.protected_fifos and fs.protected_regular switches are 2: group-writable
# sticky directories too. The README lists each refusal and the check that
# holds it.
#
# And what find may do to the tree it walks: it cannot delete (-delete) and
# cannot write a file it was told to name (-fprint, -fprint0, -fprintf), the
# two ways a walked tree's file names become a destroyed or a created file.
#
CONFIG_MOONWATER_STRICT_FILES=2
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_FIND_DELETE=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_FLOODLIGHT_FIND_WRITE=n
